body:
Right, for [CommandEvent.source](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#dom-commandevent-source) and [ToggleEvent.source](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/interaction.html#dom-toggleevent-source) this is already done -- they retarget the result against currentTarget. After dispatch, null currentTarget means the result gets retargeted to a node in the light DOM.

Hmm, it's a problem for [SubmitEvent.submitter](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/form-control-infrastructure.html#dom-submitevent-submitter) though. Browsers don't currently do any retargeting there. We could potentially make it retarget against currentTarget to match CommandEvent.source/ToggleEvent.source. This is the sort of case I'm worried about from a compat perspective though:

```html
<div id="host">
  <template shadowrootmode="open">
    <form>
      <button id="submit-button" type="submit">Submit</button>
    </form>
  </template>
</div>
<script>
const shadowRoot = document.querySelector('#host').shadowRoot;
const form = shadowRoot.querySelector('form');

form.addEventListener('submit', async (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();

  // This is the <button>:
  console.log(event.submitter);

  await new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, 0));

  // Today this is the <button>.
  // If we retarget against currentTarget in the getter like for CommandEvent and ToggleEvent, this will be <div id="host">.
  // If we use relatedTarget, it will be null.
  console.log(event.submitter);
});
</script>
```

But I guess we do have to make some breaking change for SubmitEvent. The smallest change seems to be to align it to CommandEvent/ToggleEvent by retargeting against currentTarget. Or I'm not opposed to trying to make these based on relatedTarget like we'd previously discussed, but the compat risk of that could be higher.
